CloudBurrow patch 28 to github.com/goccy/googlesqlite v0.3.1
(cloudburrow/cloudburrow#1277). Applies after patch 0027.

The bug: the SQL pre-rewriter (applyIngestionPartitionRewrite) wrote
every `_PARTITIONTIME` as CURRENT_TIMESTAMP() and every `_PARTITIONDATE`
as CURRENT_DATE(), on any table, so every row of an ingestion-time
partitioned table read as ingested at the time of the query, not at the
start of its partition, and a table that is not partitioned by ingestion
time had the pseudo-columns too. BigQuery keeps each row's ingestion
time, truncated to the table's partitioning unit (DAY, HOUR, MONTH or
YEAR), has the pseudo-columns only on an ingestion-time partitioned
table, and _PARTITIONDATE only on one partitioned by day; SELECT * does
not read them, and an INSERT may give _PARTITIONTIME
(https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/querying-partitioned-tables).

The patch gives a CREATE TABLE with a column list and PARTITION BY
_PARTITIONDATE, DATE(_PARTITIONTIME) or TIMESTAMP_TRUNC(_PARTITIONTIME,
unit) the columns _PARTITIONTIME TIMESTAMP (and, by day, _PARTITIONDATE
DATE), whose DEFAULT is the insert's time truncated to the unit (patch
0027 applies it to INSERT ... VALUES and INSERT ... SELECT); a scan
reads _PARTITIONDATE as the date of _PARTITIONTIME, so it follows an
_PARTITIONTIME an INSERT gives; the catalog makes them pseudo-columns,
which SELECT * leaves out; and references are no longer rewritten, so a
table without them fails "Unrecognized name".
ingestion_partition_test.go fails without the patch and passes with it.

